            <ArticleTitle>Cutaneous Adverse Drug Reactions to Lamotrigine and Human Leukocyte Antigen Typing in North Indian Patients: A Case Series.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>408-410</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/aian.AIAN_234_17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cutaneous adverse drug reaction (cADR) has limited epidemiological data in India. The older antiepileptic drugs, i.e., carbamazepine, phenytoin, valproic acid, phenobarbitone, etc., induce severe cADRs that have a strong associated with human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-related genetic risk factors. There is also evidence of association of certain HLA alleles with lamotrigine (LTG)-induced cADRs, but this has not been reported in the Indian population. Here, we report case series of three patients with LTG-induced &quot;Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS).&quot; Their HLA-B typing was also performed which showed the presence of HLA-B*15:02 in one case with SJS.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
